In Kubernetes:
    service to service interaction is not secure.
    Canary/traffic splitting, i.e, only few request goes to newly deployed V3 rest goes to old version v2.
        It is release strategy where you send a small percentage of traffic to a new version of a
        service before rolling it out to everyone.
    traffic retries

Envoy:
    data plane. (actual traffic)
    It actually handles traffic.
    Retry failed calls
    Apply timeouts
    Encrypt traffic (mTLS)

    Envoy A + Service A  ---->  Envoy B + Service B
    Pod
     ├── App Container
     └── Envoy Sidecar

    Kubernetes Ingress Gateway (Envoy)

Istio:
    Service Mesh Control Plane
    Configures Envoy
    Controls routing rules
    Collects telemetry, Prometheus
    Istio does NOT handle traffic itself, Envoy does.
    Istio is not Mandatory for Envoy
        We can run Envoy without Istio.
        Istio just makes Envoy manageable at scale.



Kubernetes → runs pods
Istio → controls traffic rules
Envoy → enforces traffic behavior
Our code → business logic only


Where Exactly Are Canary Rules Defined?
    📍 They are defined as Kubernetes custom resources
    👉 These live in YAML, deployed to Kubernetes, not inside a microservice.
    istioctl install
    kubectl label namespace my-ns istio-injection=enabled
    GIT/
    └── k8s/
        └── my-service/
            ├── deployment-v1.yaml
            ├── deployment-v2.yaml
            ├── service.yaml
            ├── destination-rule.yaml
            └── virtual-service.yaml

    CI/CD pipeline automatically take care of this.




